  "query_preview": "..\n\nWEATHER PATTERN OVERVIEW:\nNortheast Montana will be in NW flow aloft the next few days\nbetween an upper low over Hudson Bay and northern Manitoba and an\nupper ridge over the Rockies and Pacific NW. Temperatures will\nwarm today and Thursday ahead of a back door cold front that will\ndrop south into the forecast area Thursday afternoon. It will be a\ndry front with little or no clouds and gusty NW winds. Winds\nspeeds will be borderline for a Lake Wind Advisory for Fort Peck\nLake Thursday afternoon and evening. RH will also be borderline\nfor critical fire weather conditions. Will maintain Fire Weather\nWatch for Thursday afternoon and evening.\n\nFriday will be cooler in the north and east while the southwest \nwill have temperatures similar to Thursday being near a quasi- \nstationary front.  \n\nThe GFS and ECMWF ensembles are suggesting a flattening of the\nridge this weekend as it moves through eastern Montana. At the\nsame time, a frontal boundary will remain quasi-stationary along\nthe southern part of the forecast area so that the north will have\nhighs mainly in the 80s while the south has highs mainly in the\n90s. \n\nThe latest NBM delays any chances for precipitation until Monday\nnight across the region. \nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS:\nThere are no deviations.\n\nThere is high confidence in temperatures through the weekend.\n\nThere is low to moderate confidence in critical fire weather \noccurring Thursday afternoon.",
